      "topic": [],
      "status": 200,
      "revision": "41571179ab5100e0e44e023849cf1ae1baedb825",
      "reply": {
        "answer": "Pulse-Doppler radar measures range by timing and closure by Doppler shift, allowing it to reject clutter and track targets in velocity by canceling anything that does not move relative to the ground. A radar's horizon is the distance beyond which the earth's curve hides a low target, calculated using an effective earth radius 4/3 of the real one. Pulse repetition frequency (PRF) is the rate a radar sends pulses, which determines how far it can measure range unambiguously and how well it measures velocity. A low PRF provides accurate, long, and unambiguous range but makes velocity very hard to track; a medium PRF makes both range and velocity easy to track; and a high PRF provides excellent velocity resolution but results in ambiguous range. Radar correlation involves matching your own radar contact to a controller's call by placing the cursor and scan volume at the called bearing, range, and altitude. Additionally, a Radar Warning Receiver (RWR) is an electronic warfare support system that detects radar signals, identifies them against an emitter table, and displays them as symbols at a rough relative bearing with tones for new threats, tracks, and missile launches.",
        "ids": [
          "/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar",
